#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:3
msgid "Data Files Support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:5
msgid ""
"In the Python ecosystem, the term \"data files\" is used in various complex "
"scenarios and can have nuanced meanings. For the purposes of this "
"documentation, we define \"data files\" as non-Python files that are "
"installed alongside Python modules and packages on the user's machine when "
"they install a :term:`distribution <Distribution Package>` via :term:`wheel "
"<Wheel>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:11
msgid ""
"These files are typically intended for use at **runtime** by the package "
"itself or to influence the behavior of other packages or systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:14
msgid ""
"Old packaging installation methods in the Python ecosystem have "
"traditionally allowed installation of \"data files\", which are placed in a "
"platform-specific location.  However, the most common use case for data "
"files distributed with a package is for use *by* the package, usually by "
"including the data files **inside the package directory**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:20
msgid ""
"Setuptools focuses on this most common type of data files and offers three "
"ways of specifying which files should be included in your packages, as "
"described in the following section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:26
msgid "Configuration Options"
msgstr "組態選項"

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:32
msgid "1. ``include_package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:34
msgid "First, you can use the ``include_package_data`` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:36
msgid "For example, if the package tree looks like this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:38 ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:128
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── setup.py        # and/or setup.cfg, pyproject.toml\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── mypkg\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        ├── data1.rst\n"
"        ├── data2.rst\n"
"        ├── data1.txt\n"
"        └── data2.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:48
msgid "When **at least one** of the following conditions are met:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:50
msgid ""
"These files are included via the :ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST.in>` "
"file, like so::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:53
msgid ""
"include src/mypkg/*.txt\n"
"include src/mypkg/*.rst"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:56
msgid ""
"They are being tracked by a revision control system such as Git, Mercurial "
"or SVN, **AND** you have configured an appropriate plugin such as :pypi:"
"`setuptools-scm` or :pypi:`setuptools-svn`. (See the section below on :ref:"
"`Adding Support for Revision Control Systems` for information on how to "
"configure such plugins.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:62
msgid ""
"then all the ``.txt`` and ``.rst`` files will be included into the source "
"distribution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:65
msgid ""
"To further include them into the ``wheels``, you can use the "
"``include_package_data`` keyword:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:70
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"# ...\n"
"# By default, include-package-data is true in pyproject.toml,\n"
"# so you do NOT have to specify this line.\n"
"include-package-data = true\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:83
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"# ...\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"include_package_data = True\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:97
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    include_package_data=True\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:108
msgid ""
"The default value for ``tool.setuptools.include-package-data`` is ``true`` "
"when projects are configured via ``pyproject.toml``. This behaviour differs "
"from ``setup.cfg`` and ``setup.py`` (where ``include_package_data`` is "
"``False`` by default), which was not changed to ensure backwards "
"compatibility with existing projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:118
msgid "2. ``package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:120
msgid ""
"By default, ``include_package_data`` considers **all** non ``.py`` files "
"found inside the package directory (``src/mypkg`` in this case) as data "
"files, and includes those that satisfy (at least) one of the above two "
"conditions into the source distribution, and consequently in the "
"installation of your package. If you want finer-grained control over what "
"files are included, then you can also use the ``package_data`` keyword. For "
"example, if the package tree looks like this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:138
msgid ""
"then you can use the following configuration to capture the ``.txt`` and ``."
"rst`` files as data files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:143
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-data]\n"
"mypkg = [\"*.txt\", \"*.rst\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:153
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"# ...\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.package_data]\n"
"mypkg =\n"
"    *.txt\n"
"    *.rst"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:171
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    package_data={\"mypkg\": [\"*.txt\", \"*.rst\"]}\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:181
msgid ""
"The ``package_data`` argument is a dictionary that maps from package names "
"to lists of glob patterns. Note that the data files specified using the "
"``package_data`` option neither require to be included within a :ref:"
"`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST.in>` file, nor require to be added by a "
"revision control system plugin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:187
msgid ""
"If your glob patterns use paths, you *must* use a forward slash (``/``) as "
"the path separator, even if you are on Windows. ``setuptools`` automatically "
"converts slashes to appropriate platform-specific separators at build time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:192
msgid ""
"Glob patterns do not automatically match dotfiles, i.e., directory or file "
"names starting with a dot (``.``). To include such files, you must "
"explicitly start the pattern with a dot, e.g. ``.*`` to match ``.gitignore``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:196
msgid ""
"If you have multiple top-level packages and a common pattern of data files "
"for all these packages, for example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:199
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── setup.py        # and/or setup.cfg, pyproject.toml\n"
"└── src\n"
"    ├── mypkg1\n"
"    │   ├── data1.rst\n"
"    │   ├── data1.txt\n"
"    │   └── __init__.py\n"
"    └── mypkg2\n"
"        ├── data2.txt\n"
"        └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:210
msgid ""
"Here, both packages ``mypkg1`` and ``mypkg2`` share a common pattern of "
"having ``.txt`` data files. However, only ``mypkg1`` has ``.rst`` data "
"files. In such a case, if you want to use the ``package_data`` option, the "
"following configuration will work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:216
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-data]\n"
"\"*\" = [\"*.txt\"]\n"
"mypkg1 = [\"data1.rst\"]"
msgstr ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-data]\n"
"\"*\" = [\"*.txt\"]\n"
"mypkg1 = [\"data1.rst\"]"

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:227
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.package_data]\n"
"* =\n"
"  *.txt\n"
"mypkg1 =\n"
"  data1.rst"
msgstr ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.package_data]\n"
"* =\n"
"  *.txt\n"
"mypkg1 =\n"
"  data1.rst"

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:245
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    package_data={\"\": [\"*.txt\"], \"mypkg1\": [\"data1.rst\"]},\n"
")"
msgstr ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    package_data={\"\": [\"*.txt\"], \"mypkg1\": [\"data1.rst\"]},\n"
")"

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:255
msgid ""
"Notice that if you list patterns in ``package_data`` under the empty string "
"``\"\"`` in ``setup.py``, and the asterisk ``*`` in ``setup.cfg`` and "
"``pyproject.toml``, these patterns are used to find files in every package. "
"For example, we use ``\"\"`` or ``*`` to indicate that the ``.txt`` files "
"from all packages should be captured as data files. These placeholders are "
"treated as a special case, ``setuptools`` **do not** support glob patterns "
"on package names for this configuration (patterns are only supported on the "
"file paths). Also note how we can continue to specify patterns for "
"individual packages, i.e. we specify that ``data1.rst`` from ``mypkg1`` "
"alone should be captured as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:266
msgid ""
"When building an ``sdist``, the data files are also drawn from the "
"``package_name.egg-info/SOURCES.txt`` file which works as a form of cache. "
"So make sure that this file is removed if ``package_data`` is updated, "
"before re-building the package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:272
msgid ""
"In Python any directory is considered a package (even if it does not contain "
"``__init__.py``, see *native namespaces packages* on :doc:`PyPUG:guides/"
"packaging-namespace-packages`). Therefore, if you are not relying on :doc:"
"`automatic discovery </userguide/package_discovery>`, you *SHOULD* ensure "
"that **all** packages (including the ones that don't contain any Python "
"files) are included in the ``packages`` configuration (see :doc:`/userguide/"
"package_discovery` for more information)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:280
msgid ""
"Moreover, it is advisable to use full packages name using the dot notation "
"instead of a nested path, to avoid error prone configurations. Please check :"
"ref:`section subdirectories <subdir-data-files>` below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:288
msgid "3. ``exclude_package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:290
msgid ""
"Sometimes, the ``include_package_data`` or ``package_data`` options alone "
"aren't sufficient to precisely define what files you want included. For "
"example, consider a scenario where you have ``include_package_data=True``, "
"and you are using a revision control system with an appropriate plugin. "
"Sometimes developers add directory-specific marker files (such as ``."
"gitignore``, ``.gitkeep``, ``.gitattributes``, or ``.hgignore``), these "
"files are probably being tracked by the revision control system, and "
"therefore by default they will be included when the package is installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:299
msgid ""
"Supposing you want to prevent these files from being included in the "
"installation (they are not relevant to Python or the package), then you "
"could use the ``exclude_package_data`` option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:305
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.exclude-package-data]\n"
"mypkg = [\".gitattributes\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:315
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"# ...\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"include_package_data = True\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.exclude_package_data]\n"
"mypkg =\n"
"    .gitattributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:333
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    include_package_data=True,\n"
"    exclude_package_data={\"mypkg\": [\".gitattributes\"]},\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:344
msgid ""
"The ``exclude_package_data`` option is a dictionary mapping package names to "
"lists of wildcard patterns, just like the ``package_data`` option.  And, "
"just as with that option, you can use the empty string key ``\"\"`` in "
"``setup.py`` and the asterisk ``*`` in ``setup.cfg`` and ``pyproject.toml`` "
"to match all top-level packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:349
msgid ""
"Any files that match these patterns will be *excluded* from installation, "
"even if they were listed in ``package_data`` or were included as a result of "
"using ``include_package_data``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:356
msgid "Interplay between these keywords"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:358
msgid ""
"Meanwhile, to further clarify the interplay between these three keywords, to "
"include certain data file into the source distribution, the following logic "
"condition has to be met::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:362
msgid "MANIFEST.in or (package-data and not exclude-package-data)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:364
msgid "In plain language, the file should be either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:366
msgid "included in ``MANIFEST.in``; or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:368
msgid ""
"selected by ``package-data`` AND not excluded by ``exclude-package-data``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:370
msgid "To include some data file into the ``.whl``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:372
msgid ""
"(not exclude-package-data) and ((include-package-data and MANIFEST.in) or "
"package-data)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:374
msgid ""
"In other words, the file should not be excluded by ``exclude-package-data`` "
"(highest priority), AND should be either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:377
msgid "selected by ``package-data``; or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:379
msgid "selected by ``MANIFEST.in`` AND use ``include-package-data = true``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:382
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:384
msgid "In summary, the three options allow you to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:386
msgid "``include_package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:387
msgid ""
"Accept all data files and directories matched by :ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using "
"MANIFEST.in>` or added by a :ref:`plugin <Adding Support for Revision "
"Control Systems>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:391
msgid "``package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:392
msgid ""
"Specify additional patterns to match files that may or may not be matched "
"by :ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST.in>` or added by a :ref:`plugin <Adding "
"Support for Revision Control Systems>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:396
msgid "``exclude_package_data``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:397
msgid ""
"Specify patterns for data files and directories that should *not* be "
"included when a package is installed, even if they would otherwise have been "
"included due to the use of the preceding options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:402
msgid ""
"Due to the way the build process works, a data file that you include in your "
"project and then stop including may be \"orphaned\" in your project's build "
"directories, requiring you to manually deleting them. This may also be "
"important for your users and contributors if they track intermediate "
"revisions of your project using Subversion; be sure to let them know when "
"you make changes that remove files from inclusion so they can also manually "
"delete them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:410
msgid ""
"See also troubleshooting information in :ref:`Caching and Troubleshooting`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:416
msgid "Subdirectory for Data Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:418
msgid ""
"A common pattern is where some (or all) of the data files are placed under a "
"separate subdirectory. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:421
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── setup.py        # and/or setup.cfg, pyproject.toml\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── mypkg\n"
"        ├── data\n"
"        │   ├── data1.rst\n"
"        │   └── data2.rst\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        ├── data1.txt\n"
"        └── data2.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:432
msgid ""
"Here, the ``.rst`` files are placed under a ``data`` subdirectory inside "
"``mypkg``, while the ``.txt`` files are directly under ``mypkg``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:435
msgid ""
"In this case, the recommended approach is to treat ``data`` as a namespace "
"package (refer :pep:`420`). This way, you can rely on the same methods "
"described above, using either :ref:`package-data` or :ref:`include-package-"
"data`. For the sake of completeness, we include below configuration examples "
"for the subdirectory structure, but please refer to the detailed information "
"in the previous sections of this document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:442
msgid "With :ref:`package-data`, the configuration might look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:446
msgid ""
"# Scanning for namespace packages in the ``src`` directory is true by\n"
"# default in pyproject.toml, so you do NOT need to include the\n"
"# `tool.setuptools.packages.find` if it looks like the following:\n"
"# [tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"# namespaces = true\n"
"# where = [\"src\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-data]\n"
"mypkg = [\"*.txt\"]\n"
"\"mypkg.data\" = [\"*.rst\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:461
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"# ...\n"
"packages = find_namespace:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"\n"
"[options.package_data]\n"
"mypkg =\n"
"    *.txt\n"
"mypkg.data =\n"
"    *.rst"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:480
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_namespace_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    packages=find_namespace_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    package_data={\n"
"        \"mypkg\": [\"*.txt\"],\n"
"        \"mypkg.data\": [\"*.rst\"],\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:493
msgid ""
"In other words, we allow ``setuptools`` to scan for namespace packages in "
"the ``src`` directory, which enables the ``data`` directory to be "
"identified, and then, we separately specify data files for the root package "
"``mypkg``, and the namespace package ``data`` under the package ``mypkg``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:498
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can also rely on :ref:`include-package-data`. Note that "
"this is the default behaviour in ``pyproject.toml``, but you need to "
"manually enable scanning of namespace packages in ``setup.cfg`` or ``setup."
"py``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:504
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"# ...\n"
"# By default, include-package-data is true in pyproject.toml, so you do\n"
"# NOT have to specify this line.\n"
"include-package-data = true\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"# scanning for namespace packages is true by default in pyproject.toml, so\n"
"# you need NOT include this configuration.\n"
"namespaces = true\n"
"where = [\"src\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:520
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find_namespace:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"include_package_data = True\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:533
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_namespace_packages\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ... ,\n"
"    packages=find_namespace_packages(where=\"src\"),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"},\n"
"    include_package_data=True,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:543
msgid ""
"To avoid common mistakes with :ref:`include-package-data`, please ensure :"
"ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST.in>` is properly set or use a revision "
"control system plugin (see :doc:`/userguide/miscellaneous`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:551
msgid "Accessing Data Files at Runtime"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:553
msgid ""
"Typically, existing programs manipulate a package's ``__file__`` attribute "
"in order to find the location of data files. For example, if you have a "
"structure like this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:557
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── setup.py        # and/or setup.cfg, pyproject.toml\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── mypkg\n"
"        ├── data\n"
"        │   └── data1.txt\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        └── foo.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:566
msgid ""
"Then, in ``mypkg/foo.py``, you may try something like this in order to "
"access ``mypkg/data/data1.txt``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:569
msgid ""
"import os\n"
"data_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), 'data', 'data1.txt')\n"
"with open(data_path, 'r') as data_file:\n"
"     ..."
msgstr ""
"import os\n"
"data_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), 'data', 'data1.txt')\n"
"with open(data_path, 'r') as data_file:\n"
"     ..."

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:576
msgid ""
"However, this manipulation isn't compatible with :pep:`302`-based import "
"hooks, including importing from zip files and Python Eggs.  It is strongly "
"recommended that, if you are using data files, you should use :mod:"
"`importlib.resources` to access them. In this case, you would do something "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:581
msgid ""
"from importlib.resources import files\n"
"data_text = files('mypkg.data').joinpath('data1.txt').read_text()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:586
msgid ""
":mod:`importlib.resources` was added to Python 3.7. However, the API "
"illustrated in this code (using ``files()``) was added only in Python 3.9, "
"[#files_api]_ and support for accessing data files via namespace packages "
"was added only in Python 3.10 [#namespace_support]_ (the ``data`` "
"subdirectory is a namespace package under the root package ``mypkg``). "
"Therefore, you may find this code to work only in Python 3.10 (and above). "
"For other versions of Python, you are recommended to use the :pypi:"
"`importlib-resources` backport which provides the latest version of this "
"library. In this case, the only change that has to be made to the above code "
"is to replace ``importlib.resources`` with ``importlib_resources``, i.e."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:595
msgid ""
"from importlib_resources import files\n"
"..."
msgstr ""
"from importlib_resources import files\n"
"..."

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:600
msgid "See :doc:`importlib-resources:using` for detailed instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:602
msgid ""
"Files inside the package directory should be *read-only* to avoid a series "
"of common problems (e.g. when multiple users share a common Python "
"installation, when the package is loaded from a zip file, or when multiple "
"instances of a Python application run in parallel)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:607
msgid ""
"If your Python package needs to write to a file for shared data or "
"configuration, you can use standard platform/OS-specific system directories, "
"such as ``~/.local/config/$appname`` or ``/usr/share/$appname/$version`` "
"(Linux specific) [#system-dirs]_. A common approach is to add a read-only "
"template file to the package directory that is then copied to the correct "
"system directory if no pre-existing file is found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:616
msgid "Data Files from Plugins and Extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:618
msgid ""
"You can resort to a :doc:`native/implicit namespace package <PyPUG:guides/"
"packaging-namespace-packages>` (as a container for files) if you want "
"plugins and extensions to your package to contribute with package data "
"files. This way, all files will be listed during runtime when :doc:`using "
"importlib.resources <importlib-resources:using>`. Note that, although not "
"strictly guaranteed, mainstream Python package managers, like :pypi:`pip` "
"and derived tools, will install files belong to multiple distributions that "
"share a same namespace into the same directory in the file system. This "
"means that the overhead for :mod:`importlib.resources` will be minimum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:630
msgid "Non-Package Data Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:632
msgid ""
"Historically, ``setuptools`` by way of ``easy_install`` would encapsulate "
"data files from the distribution into the egg (see `the old docs <https://"
"github.com/pypa/setuptools/blob/52aacd5b276fedd6849c3a648a0014f5da563e93/"
"docs/setuptools.txt#L970-L1001>`_). As eggs are deprecated and pip-based "
"installs fall back to the platform-specific location for installing data "
"files, there is no supported facility to reliably retrieve these resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:638
msgid ""
"Instead, the PyPA recommends that any data files you wish to be accessible "
"at run time be included **inside the package**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/datafiles.rst:644
msgid ""
"These locations can be discovered with the help of third-party libraries "
"such as :pypi:`platformdirs`."
msgstr ""

